    "case_id": "604",
    "case_summary": "A 64-year-old man with a medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypothyroidism, and hypertension presented with a 1-month onset of worsening dyspnea and unintentional 10-lb weight loss.\n\nPatient was afebrile, had a blood pressure of 100/60 mm Hg, respiratory rate of 42/min, heart rate of 110 beats/min, and oxygen saturation 77% on room air. Physical examination revealed bilateral vesicular breathing with diffuse rhonchi on lung auscultation; no lymphadenopathy was identified.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Hemoglobin: 11.2 g/dL    * Leukocyte: 10.2 ×10³/mcL    * Platelets: 410 ×10³/mcL    * C-reactive protein: 23.4 mg/dL    * Lactate dehydrogenase: 280 EnzU/L    * Other tests: Respiratory viral panel, SARS-CoV-2 PCR, fungal pneumonia work-up were negative. QuantiFERON TB test, hepatitis panel, and HIV were nonreactive.  - Imaging Studies:    * CT Chest: Showed innumerable ill-defined nodular opacities within both lungs, air bronchogram pattern, superimposed pneumonia, and an ill-defined 6.3 × 5.8-cm right hilar mass narrowing the right central bronchi    * CT Abdomen/Pelvis: Showed splenomegaly    * PET scan: Showed marked FDG-avid uptake at the lung lesion, right neck lymph node, and left acetabular bone marrow, with a splenic focus  - Pathology:    * CT-guided lung biopsy: Revealed patchy areas of large atypical lymphoid cells with necrosis    * VATS wedge resection: Showed infiltration of lung parenchyma by malignant neoplasm, mixed inflammatory infiltrate, marked nuclear pleomorphism with atypical mitotic figures, vascular involvement, and areas of parenchymal necrosis",
    "frontier": [
      "B1",
      "B2",
      "B3"
    ],
